        "target": "Given that 40% of the farmer's cattle are males and he has 50 male cows, I first needed to determine the total number of cattle. I used the equation:\n\\[ 0.40 \\times T = 50 \\]\nwhere \\( T \\) is the total number of cattle. Solving for \\( T \\):\n\\[ T = \\frac{50}{0.40} = 125 \\]\nThis means the total number of cattle is 125. Since 40% are males, the remaining 60% must be females. Therefore, the number of female cows is:\n\\[ 125 - 50 = 75 \\]\nEach female cow produces 2 gallons of milk a day, so the total milk produced by the female cows is:\n\\[ 75 \\times 2 = 150 \\]\nThis calculation shows that the farmer will get 150 gallons of milk a day from the female cows. To verify, I checked the total number of cattle and the percentage of males, ensuring the arithmetic was correct. The operations used were straightforward percentage calculations and multiplication.\n\nThe reusable operation here is the calculation of the total number of cattle from the given percentage and the number of males, and then using that total to find the number of females and their milk production. This operation can be reused in similar problems involving percentages and proportional relationships.\n\nFINAL: 150",
